{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6e6e2b412266145","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9008\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9008\n11\t\n12\tMake RHOAI more usable for enterprise teams.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage Outcome\n15\t\n16\tNeither Step 0 check fires; this strategy gets a full multi-epic decomposition:\n17\t\n18\t- **Not below-threshold**: the strategy contains three genuine unknowns (its own\n19\t  Open Questions section) whose answers change which epics exist and what they\n20\t  do — the HLR-1 requirement is self-contradictory (one account wants fewer\n21\t  exposed configuration options, another wants more fine-grained per-project\n22\t  control), HLR-2's scope is undefined, and HLR-3 names no tools. It also spans\n23\t  multiple work streams (usability investigation + code implementation across\n24\t  two components + substantive whitepaper authoring).\n25\t- **Not docs-only**: Dashboard and Notebooks are active code components; only\n26\t  HLR-4 is documentation content.\n27\t\n28\t## Strategy Parsing Notes\n29\t\n30\t- No Staff Engineer Input section is present — no overrides to log.\n31\t- **Missing sections (health warnings, decomposition proceeded with available\n32\t  information):** Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table, Acceptance\n33\t  Criteria, Non-Functional Requirements, and Technical Approach are all absent.\n34\t- Affected Components are listed without change descriptions or owners. Owning\n35\t  teams were derived from `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`\n36\t  (Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard; Notebooks → Workbenches). Both components exist\n37\t  in the platform (architecture context files present, both Konflux-onboarded),\n38\t  so no onboarding chain is generated.\n39\t- HLR-1, HLR-2, and HLR-3 carry no priority in the strategy. They are assigned\n40\t  **P1** here on the strength of \"leadership wants visible usability progress\n41\t  this release\" — not P0 (nothing is release-blocking or an outage) and not P2\n42\t  (the unprioritized HLRs are the point of the strategy, unlike the explicitly\n43\t  P2 whitepaper). This is a judgment call, flagged in Health Warnings.\n44\t\n45\t## Epic List\n46\t\n47\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n48\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n49\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E001 | Resolve the conflicting enterprise requirements for admin configuration | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n50\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E002 | Define data-scientist onboarding scope and locate where the onboarding day is spent | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n51\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E003 | Identify the enterprise tools meant by the integration feedback | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n52\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E004 | Deliver the resolved admin-configuration experience | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n53\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E005 | Cut new-data-scientist onboarding time to under a day | Implementation | Workbenches | P1 |\n54\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E006 | Integrate RHOAI with the identified enterprise tools | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n55\t| RHAISTRAT-9008-E007 | Publish the enterprise usability best-practices whitepaper | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | P2 |\n56\t\n57\t## Dependency DAG\n58\t\n59\t```mermaid\n60\tgraph TD\n61\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9008-E001<br/>Investigation: admin-config conflict] --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9008-E004<br/>Implementation: admin-config experience]\n62\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9008-E002<br/>Investigation: onboarding scope/time] --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9008-E005<br/>Implementation: onboarding time]\n63\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9008-E003<br/>Investigation: enterprise tools] --> E006[RHAISTRAT-9008-E006<br/>Implementation: tool integrations]\n64\t    E004 --> E007[RHAISTRAT-9008-E007<br/>docs-authoring: whitepaper]\n65\t    E005 --> E007\n66\t    E006 --> E007\n67\t```\n68\t\n69\tRoots (E001, E002, E003) are independent investigations that start in\n70\tparallel; each gates exactly one Implementation; the whitepaper is blocked by\n71\tall three Implementations (Rule 11, docs always last).\n72\t\n73\t## DAG Justification\n74\t\n75\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n76\t|------|------|-----------|\n77\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The HLR-1 conflict (fewer knobs vs. fine-grained controls) cannot be resolved by reading code or docs — it needs the scattered account feedback consolidated and a precedence decision made. The answer rewrites E004's entire scope, so E004 is a true gate (`gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`, `gated_by=E001`). |\n78\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | What \"onboarding\" covers and where the day is spent determines whether E005 targets workbench startup, console UX, cluster access, or project setup — i.e., which epic exists at all. True gate (`rewrite`), `gated_by=E002`. |\n79\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | No tools are named; until the feedback is consolidated and tools identified, there is nothing to integrate. True gate (`rewrite`), `gated_by=E003`. |\n80\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | The whitepaper describes enterprise usability best practices and must reflect what was actually built; docs are always last. Docs-authoring priority exemption applies (E007 stays P2; no priority inheritance). |\n81\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same as above. |\n82\t| E006 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same as above. |\n83\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 analog (independent investigations) | The three unknowns are independent: feedback-conflict resolution, onboarding time analysis, and tool identification neither consume each other's outputs nor share deliverables. They are separate Investigation epics per Step 3 (distinct unknowns, distinct deliverables), run in parallel. E001 and E003 share a feedback-consolidation *method* (both start from the scattered field feedback) and should coordinate, but their deliverables and gated work differ — a decision record vs. a prioritized tool list — so they are not merged. |\n84\t\n85\tNo `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics: both\n86\taffected components are in the platform and Konflux-onboarded per architecture\n87\tcontext. No external-dependency Implementation epics: no upstream PR/RFC is\n88\trequired by anything stated in the strategy.\n89\t\n90\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n91\t\n92\t| HLR | Priority (strategy) | Priority (assigned) | Epics | Coverage |\n93\t|-----|--------------------|--------------------|-------|----------|\n94\t| HLR-1 — simpler administration (conflicted: fewer knobs vs. more fine-grained controls) | — | P1 | E001 (resolve conflict), E004 (implement resolved direction) | Full — the requirement is inherently two-step: decide, then build |\n95\t| HLR-2 — onboarding a new data scientist in under a day | — | P1 | E002 (define scope, locate time cost), E005 (implement reduction) | Full |\n96\t| HLR-3 — better integration with existing enterprise tooling | — | P1 | E003 (identify tools), E006 (implement integrations) | Full |\n97\t| HLR-4 — publish enterprise usability best-practices whitepaper | P2 | P2 | E007 (docs-authoring) | Full; priority derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Normal` → P2 |\n98\t\n99\tNo orphaned HLRs. No redundant/overlapping HLRs — HLR-1/2/3/4 describe four\n100\tdistinct capabilities.\n101\t\n102\t## Component Mapping Notes\n103\t\n104\t- `Dashboard` — assigned to E001, E002, E003, E004, E006. The admin-configuration\n105\t  surfaces (per-component feature flags and defaults via `OdhDashboardConfig`),\n106\t  the onboarding entry point (project setup, workbench spawn UI), and any\n107\t  enterprise-tool integration surface through the single-pane console are all\n108\t  Dashboard territory per architecture context. E002's and E003's work spans\n109\t  the whole platform journey, but the Dashboard team is the nearest active\n110\t  owner for both studies.\n111\t- `Notebooks` — assigned to E005. The onboarding milestone ends at \"first\n112\t  workbench\" (open question 2 enumerates it); the workbench lifecycle\n113\t  (notebook-controller, spawn defaults, culling) is owned by the Workbenches\n114\t  team. If E002 locates the time cost outside the workbench experience, E005's\n115\t  `gate_failure_impact` rewrite covers re-targeting or re-assignment.\n116\t- `Documentation` — assigned to E007, the whitepaper content deliverable. No\n117\t  architecture-context file exists for Documentation; the team (`RHOAI Docs`)\n118\t  is an assumption flagged in Health Warnings.\n119\t\n120\t## Health Warnings\n121\t\n122\t1. **Strategy is materially under-specified.** No Impacted Teams, Dependencies,\n123\t   Acceptance Criteria, NFRs, or Technical Approach sections. Teams and ACs in\n124\t   this decomposition are derived from architecture context and the problem\n125\t   statement; a human should confirm team assignments (especially E007's) before\n126\t   execution.\n127\t2. **HLR-1 is self-contradictory** (\"too many knobs\" vs. \"more fine-grained\n128\t   controls per project\"). This is resolved by E001 before any implementation\n129\t   starts; implementing either direction before the precedence decision would\n130\t   risk building the wrong thing for a strategic account.\n131\t3. **Priority inversion risk (assignment judgment).** HLR-1/2/3 carry no\n132\t   priority in the strategy. They are assigned P1 here because leadership wants\n133\t   visible progress this release. If the owning stakeholders disagree, the\n134\t   priorities of E001-E006 should be re-triaged together (they were kept uniform\n135\t   so no artificial ordering is introduced between the parallel branches).\n136\t4. **Scope trap — nothing is scoped out.** \"Nothing has been explicitly scoped\n137\t   out\" combined with three open-ended HLRs means this strategy could absorb\n138\t   unbounded work. The decomposition bounds it: one gated implementation epic\n139\t   per HLR, scope frozen at the functional level stated in the strategy. The\n140\t   implementing teams must resist scope growth inside those epics.\n141\t5. **Premise risk — feedback is unconsolidated.** The problem statement itself\n142\t   says feedback \"arrived through different channels and has not been\n143\t   consolidated\" and is \"partially contradictory\". E001/E003 both begin by\n144\t   consolidating it; if the consolidated corpus contradicts the strategy's\n145\t   framing, the gated implementations will be rewritten (covered by their\n146\t   `gate_failure_impact`).\n147\t6. **Out-of-scope vacuum.** With no explicit exclusions, NFRs (none stated)\n148\t   cannot contradict scope — but the absence of NFRs means the implementation\n149\t   epics carry no performance/reliability bars beyond what their ACs state.\n150\t\n151\t## Tiered Delivery\n152\t\n153\tNot applicable in the Step 5.5 sense: there is no external dependency with\n154\tuncertain timing (no upstream PR/RFC or vendor deliverable gates anything).\n155\tHowever, the strategy is naturally **phased**: Tier 1 is the three\n156\tinvestigations (risk-reduction — they resolve every unknown before code is\n157\twritten), Tier 2 is the three gated implementations, and the whitepaper lands\n158\tlast. Each branch (E001→E004→E007-branch, etc.) delivers its findings or value\n159\tindependently of the other two.\n160\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a65da344f1754f2a","agent_id":"a2f6433638117d63b","session_id":"8ed8530c-ec7e-4422-9e2a-22ede41c26ec","uuid":"9e6aa440-897b-4a9a-b2d1-ef876970bcba","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:46:43.621Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6e6e2b412266145","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9008 decomposition"}
