r architecture context), so rules 7/8/10 parallelism\nconstraints are vacuously satisfied. The three investigation roots run in\nparallel — nothing serializes naturally-parallel work. Critical path length 3\n(investigation → implementation → whitepaper) is appropriate for a\nmedium-size strategy. Cross-checking epic content for un-captured data\ndependencies: E007's \"Measurement guidance\" draws on investigation baselines,\nbut those reach E007 transitively through the implementation chain each\ninvestigation gates, so no direct edge is missing. E001/E003 coordination is a\nmethod-level note, not an artifact dependency.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nDistinct component/team tuples produce separate epics (Dashboard/RHOAI\nDashboard, Notebooks/Workbenches, Documentation/RHOAI Docs). No epic bundles\nmultiple teams. Implementation epics explicitly restrict documentation work to\nincidental touch-ups (\"no separate content effort\"), and all content\nauthoring is isolated in E007 — no work-product type mixing. No sibling epics\nbuild the same deliverable. Two minor observations (below threshold):\n- E001 and E003 each include consolidating the same unconsolidated\n  field-feedback corpus; E003's scope explicitly coordinates \"rather than\n  duplicating it\" and the deliverables differ (decision record vs. tool\n  shortlist), but a single shared consolidation step would avoid residual\n  duplicated effort.\n- E002 is a platform-wide onboarding study assigned to the Dashboard team as\n  \"nearest active owner\" — an acknowledged assumption (Health Warnings)\n  that a human should confirm before execution.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nAll three Investigation epics genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes\ndownstream structure: each gated implementation's scope is unwritable until\nthe investigation lands (conflict precedence, onboarding cost centers, tool\nidentity). All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Gating\nmetadata is complete and correct: E0